  "summary": "Bead-and-shell maps of Luba history: boards that only royal memory men can read.",
  "description": "The lukasa is a carved wooden board — often shaped like a turtle or a woman — studded with beads, shells and incised lines that encode Luba royal genealogy, migration routes and secret society knowledge. Its \"readers\" (mbudye society members) hold it in the left hand and trace bead clusters with a finger, each touch unlocking a chapter of recited history; the same board supports different readings for different occasions. Museums display them as sculpture; the mbudye understood them as libraries.",
  "culturalContext": "The story of the lukasa is closely associated with the history of the Luba kingdom, which dominated most of northern Shaba during the eighteenth century and the first half of the nineteenth century. No candidate for political office could receive his title without first becoming a member of the Bambudye society, and the ruler of the Luba kingdom held the highest ranking Bambudye title. Reefe states that while it is not possible to date the origins of the lukasa, the high degree of integration of the lukasa into the structure of the Bambudye society and into the oral lore of the Luba kingdom strongly suggests that this art form is of considerable antiquity.",
